            <ArticleTitle>[Low back pain among video-terminal workers: ergonomic postural interventions].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>16-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Several studies report a high prevalence of low back pain (LBP) among video-terminal (VDT) workers. Targeted ergonomic interventions can help reduce the burden.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVES" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">We investigated the long-term efficacy of an ergonomic postural intervention to address LBP among VDT workers.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="STUDY DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Non-randomized, crossover trial.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="POPULATION AND SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">Four hundred employees working in the administrative offices of the two main buildings of the town hall ofForlì who used VDTs for at least 20 hours a week; we randomly selected 100 participants from each building.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="INTERVENTION" NlmCategory="METHODS">Ergonomic adjustment of the VDT workstation design.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OUTCOME MEASURES" NlmCategory="METHODS">Changes in prevalence of LBP and work-related posture.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The ergonomic adjustment of the workstation improved work-related posture and reduced LBP in VDT operators who benefited from the intervention.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">A personalized ergonomic prevention intervention can improve work-related posture and LBP for VDT workers.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
